#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184146 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dce948b0-e63a-5dcc-a33a-e68492b98321" :knowledge-point/title "Mixed Numbers by Proper Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "71291025-e9b3-55b1-9de2-1e6be8310476" :question/math-academy-id "e-14571" :question/problem "A field has an area of $2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ acres. If a farmer wants to use $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ of the field to grow corn, how much of the field would be used to grow corn?" :question/worked-solution "To find out the area used to grow corn, we need to calculate $2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5}.$ \n\nFirst, we write $2 \\,\\dfrac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 3) + 2}{3} = \\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{\\color{red}3} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}3$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}3$) have a common factor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n8\\div 5 = 1\\,\\textrm{R} \\, 3 = 1\\,\\dfrac{3}{5} \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, $1\\,\\dfrac{3}{5}$ acres would be used to grow corn."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184147 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88656f-353a-5d5a-88f7-c85a5c9eec3e" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "22c9774c-400e-5baf-a874-fda211b2bb61" :question/math-academy-id "e-14572" :question/problem "A chocolate bar is made with $1\\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\\,\\textrm{oz}$ of cocoa. If Casey has $2\\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ bars, how many ounces of cocoa did it take to make them?" :question/worked-solution "To find out how many ounces of cocoa it took to make the chocolate bars, we need to calculate $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}.$    \n\nFirst, we write $1\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 4) + 1}{4}= \\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}=\\dfrac{(2 \\times 3)+2}{3}= \\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}4} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}8}{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}8$) have a common factor of $4.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 2}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10}{3} =3\\,\\textrm{R}\\, 1 = 3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, it took $3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{oz}$ of cocoa to make Casey's chocolate bars."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b3a2302-d863-584a-acd6-1025ff362a04" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "5a6ee672-4f62-5fc1-8285-b30ba76e955d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14573" :question/problem "Customers are served every $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ minutes at a particular bank. How long does it take to serve eight customers?" :question/worked-solution "To find out how long it takes to serve eight customers, we need to multiply $2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}$ by $8.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8 = \\dfrac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\color{red}4} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}8}{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}8$) have a common \nfactor of $4.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{1} & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times 2 \\\\  & =9\\times 2 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes $18$ minutes to serve $8$ customers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b3a2302-d863-584a-acd6-1025ff362a04" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bce7c2c-3cb9-58c1-8a84-8000fa8f71af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501488} {:answer/id #uuid "2b25eb19-bb5d-5b3e-ba1e-9236597e57a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501543} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a8c3c3-b1ce-5a94-bbb7-0d2565ba4c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501780} {:answer/id #uuid "c33617b4-bd39-53c7-a9c3-703ec4bf8bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501855} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d56cab-7864-5573-8143-a97b2d71cecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c33617b4-bd39-53c7-a9c3-703ec4bf8bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501855} :answer-field/id #uuid "8af8a66b-1ac8-5cdc-8665-c6b9c6575729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42bf2d9b-a245-5919-95ec-686175cfea53" :question/math-academy-id "q-193555" :question/problem "Mario fills three bottles with water. If the capacity of every bottle is $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ liters, how much water did he use to fill the bottles?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the total volume of water used to fill the bottles, we need to multiply $3$ by $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($3$) have a common factor of $3.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mario used $4$ liters of water to fill the bottles."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b3a2302-d863-584a-acd6-1025ff362a04" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef7806d-fccb-5849-a7b6-053e4643b298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501580} {:answer/id #uuid "60cd3f11-ed12-5597-869e-0704c4145a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501646} {:answer/id #uuid "689a4c42-ec46-5fb6-8385-9e072e54eb2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501658} {:answer/id #uuid "92fb21d5-96d0-54c8-91f9-5f207e357e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501748} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e48f42-1be4-5a71-8f7e-2ca629c747e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ef7806d-fccb-5849-a7b6-053e4643b298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501580} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97851e5-e9aa-5018-a200-c45258f97324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17d541e7-21d6-5b63-bfea-03f360c417cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-193579" :question/problem "Daniel reads $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ chapters of a book every hour. How many chapters does he read in $3$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "To find out the number of chapters Daniel reads, we need to multiply $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ by $3.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($3$) and the second fraction's numerator ($3$) have a common factor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1} & =\\frac{7}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1}\\times 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Daniel reads $7$ chapters in three hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b3a2302-d863-584a-acd6-1025ff362a04" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5792a8b4-8924-5147-9d55-c30ccd005655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$13\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501629} {:answer/id #uuid "74f300fc-a4cc-5903-b3a9-c5c9c3e1f4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501682} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce1b0b9-e11a-5126-81c3-75bdd87e6fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501700} {:answer/id #uuid "91b7ccf3-5bfc-5ce8-919f-a98b77646a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501744} {:answer/id #uuid "dac1018b-4417-5222-82ba-3c287ae4438a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5792a8b4-8924-5147-9d55-c30ccd005655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$13\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501629} :answer-field/id #uuid "3803c5e8-e767-518e-86a8-972773da6c8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4e556544-bb4a-576d-8275-871131562662" :question/math-academy-id "q-193605" :question/problem "Nancy uses $2\\frac{1}{4}$ cups of flour to make a dozen cupcakes. How many cups of flour does she need to prepare an order for $6$ dozen cupcakes?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the amount of flour that Nancy needs, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 6.$\n\nFirst, we write  $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1} & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{2}=13\\,\\text{R}\\,1=13\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, Nancy needs $13\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups of flour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184147 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88656f-353a-5d5a-88f7-c85a5c9eec3e" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cf7c18b-415b-5ab1-87c5-2ef3d61e9275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8\\,\\frac{5}{8}$ acres" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501606} {:answer/id #uuid "59bc80e5-3d2d-56c6-99d8-a8c4a7fa8df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7\\,\\frac{7}{8}$ acres" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501634} {:answer/id #uuid "a00a6840-b57b-5199-b23e-1052928bfb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7\\,\\frac{3}{8}$ acres" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501765} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf0e212-f4b4-50d3-9b43-dd1bd5dbf1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7\\,\\frac{5}{8}$ acres" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501803} {:answer/id #uuid "aff326ea-fe93-5341-8889-ae5d6b9507b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8\\,\\frac{3}{8}$ acres" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59bc80e5-3d2d-56c6-99d8-a8c4a7fa8df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7\\,\\frac{7}{8}$ acres" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501634} :answer-field/id #uuid "4df7ed25-2b81-5abe-a336-102de5ca8d6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35b950d1-e08b-5f6b-af7f-1977577a5d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-212001" :question/problem "A farmer has five $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$-acre fields. He planted wheat in $3$ fields and in $\\frac{1}{2}$ of another field. How many acres of wheat did the farmer plant?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the farmer planted wheat in $3+\\frac{1}{2}=3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ fields. So, to find out the total acres, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 3\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{9\\times 7}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{63}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{63}{8}=7\\,\\text{R}\\,7=7\\,\\frac{7}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, the farmer planted $7\\,\\frac{7}{8}$ acres of wheat."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184146 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dce948b0-e63a-5dcc-a33a-e68492b98321" :knowledge-point/title "Mixed Numbers by Proper Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e6a7d5-71e0-5bbc-821b-dbe1461bb350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501513} {:answer/id #uuid "57da555e-68b6-5fa2-b13b-dee74f0e9b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501631} {:answer/id #uuid "7b25dd3f-f250-5233-ac64-082fc0ce89df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501694} {:answer/id #uuid "96ff3fe5-bc70-55f3-823c-889e467c771f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501752} {:answer/id #uuid "d76ffbc9-7805-5ab5-bba9-30ddcd54b0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e6a7d5-71e0-5bbc-821b-dbe1461bb350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501513} :answer-field/id #uuid "923dd959-2580-5ffb-bb54-f2de4358652b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99bffa2e-8eed-5bab-aa2e-ce6936e0836c" :question/math-academy-id "q-60119" :question/problem "Fred is $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft}$ tall. Julie is $\\frac{4}{5}$ of Fred's height. How tall is Julie?" :question/worked-solution "To find out Julie's height, we need to calculate $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFirst, we write $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(4\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($2$) and the second fraction's numerator ($4$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 2}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting this to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n18÷5=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, Julie is $3\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{ft}$ tall."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184146 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dce948b0-e63a-5dcc-a33a-e68492b98321" :knowledge-point/title "Mixed Numbers by Proper Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145fc0da-b7aa-5abe-8ab2-177b4bf37048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501505} {:answer/id #uuid "23b745be-1845-5881-ae0c-4d6185edc055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{5}{8}\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501528} {:answer/id #uuid "3800f64f-a7fd-5fab-a59c-c39bbfe1c084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501567} {:answer/id #uuid "c8eb3508-f619-5116-9486-e669b794a1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501866} {:answer/id #uuid "f26b7497-f839-5917-8900-4c877fea7302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f26b7497-f839-5917-8900-4c877fea7302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501953} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4270c3-9d05-590f-ac4c-442b755be0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c7467de-330b-5939-83a9-4571e046a2da" :question/math-academy-id "q-60535" :question/problem "A country road is $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mi}$ long. If $\\frac{3}{4}$ of it is paved with asphalt, how long is the paved part?" :question/worked-solution "To find out the length of the paved part of the road, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{4}.$ \n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(2\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5\\times 3}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n15÷8=1\\,\\text{R}\\,7=1\\,\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the paved part of the road is $1\\,\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{mi}$ long."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184147 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88656f-353a-5d5a-88f7-c85a5c9eec3e" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ff12d1-0b1c-5615-8879-4f1628cbf97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{5}{8}\\,\\text{oz}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501514} {:answer/id #uuid "34d3b599-fe88-57be-8f39-d8fc532b0060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{oz}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501562} {:answer/id #uuid "80c0f712-b0d7-53bd-9f2f-3a8efb1b1cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{oz}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501712} {:answer/id #uuid "9dad3397-6218-5978-83c9-3f31dd0a3330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{oz}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501761} {:answer/id #uuid "e38fc58a-3f60-52da-a6a5-b7ac046c04d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{oz}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34d3b599-fe88-57be-8f39-d8fc532b0060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{oz}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501562} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff1157bb-0fbe-5352-aacf-b764d0f6e0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bd87de3-6afe-54f3-8697-30d835d51e37" :question/math-academy-id "q-60605" :question/problem "A box of cornflakes cereal weighs $1\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{oz}.$ Last week, Debby ate $2$ full boxes and another box which was only $\\frac{1}{4}$ full. How many ounces of this cereal did Debby eat in total?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that Debby had $2+\\frac{1}{4}=2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ boxes of cornflakes. So, to find out the total weight, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 2\\,\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(1\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 9}{2\\times 8}=\\frac{27}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{8}=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, Debby ate a total of $3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{oz}$ of cornflakes cereal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184147 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88656f-353a-5d5a-88f7-c85a5c9eec3e" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156bdc6e-3d42-5028-bc35-b5559648827e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501509} {:answer/id #uuid "69cfebc1-0895-5011-b698-23c2db2dafb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501660} {:answer/id #uuid "7b97cea5-6bcf-5b68-975e-af70b010accc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501696} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d2ab0b-a96a-5cb6-af20-ac52c6e2a6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501927} {:answer/id #uuid "fbea6d94-3174-559e-8095-4488656b9a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbea6d94-3174-559e-8095-4488656b9a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501967} :answer-field/id #uuid "390deacb-80eb-5c32-93dc-2caad56c72e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "222813e8-d21a-59ba-b697-7bcb16bc7844" :question/math-academy-id "q-60612" :question/problem "A can of bitter lemon drink contains $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. If Phil drank $1$ whole can and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of a second can, how many grams of sugar did he drink?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that Phil drank a total of $1+\\frac{3}{5}=1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ cans of the beverage. So, to find out how many grams of sugar he drank, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\times 1\\,\\frac{3}{5}.$    \n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($5$) and the second fraction's denominator ($5$) have a common factor of $5.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{5} & =\\frac{5}{5}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, Phil drank $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar."}]}
